---
// Static, hand-drawn illustrative diagrams, generated from a real, honestly-fit example (a small
// sample regression, not simulated live data, the live tool below computes for real). Labeled as
// sketches so they're never mistaken for the actual computed output.
---

<section id="core-idea" class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-6 py-16 md:px-12 md:py-20">
	<h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight md:text-3xl">Why does removing noise help?</h2>
	<p class="mt-3 max-w-[65ch] text-base leading-relaxed text-ink">
		Think of a kitchen scale's <strong>tare</strong> button. Before you weigh an ingredient, you set the bowl on the
		scale and press tare, it subtracts the bowl's weight so the reading shows only the ingredient. The bowl's weight
		was never what you cared about, it was just noise sitting on top of the number you actually wanted. CUPED does
		the same thing to an experiment metric: it subtracts out the part of a visitor's outcome that was already
		predictable from what you knew about them before the experiment even started, so what's left reflects the
		treatment more clearly.
	</p>
	<p class="mt-3 max-w-[65ch] text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
		It's a personalized tare, though, not a flat one. A <strong>golf handicap</strong> is the closer analogy for that
		part: a handicap doesn't subtract the same number of strokes from everyone, it adjusts each player against their
		own known baseline, so a beginner's 95 and a scratch golfer's 78 can both be judged as "how much did you improve
		today," not just "who shot the lower number." CUPED adjusts each visitor against their own pre-period baseline,
		not a single number for the whole experiment.
	</p>

	<div class="mt-10 grid grid-cols-1 gap-10 lg:grid-cols-2">
		<figure>
			<figcaption class="font-display text-lg font-semibold">The same real difference, easier to see with less noise</figcaption>
			<svg viewBox="0 0 300 200" class="mt-3 w-full" role="img" aria-label="Two overlapping bell curves representing arms A and B before adjustment, and the same two curves narrower and more clearly separated after adjustment.">
				<text x="150" y="16" text-anchor="middle" class="font-mono" font-size="11" fill="var(--color-ink-soft)">before (raw metric)</text>
				<path d="M25,170 C46,170 61.5,79 115,70 C168.5,79 184,170 205,170" fill="none" stroke="var(--color-ink-soft)" stroke-width="2" opacity="0.8" />
				<path d="M85,170 C106,170 121.5,79 175,70 C228.5,79 244,170 265,170" fill="none" stroke="var(--color-accent-blue)" stroke-width="2" opacity="0.8" />
				<line x1="115" y1="170" x2="115" y2="70" stroke="var(--color-ink-soft)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="2 3" />
				<line x1="175" y1="170" x2="175" y2="70" stroke="var(--color-accent-blue)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="2 3" />
				<text x="115" y="185" text-anchor="middle" class="font-mono" font-size="10" fill="var(--color-ink-soft)">A</text>
				<text x="175" y="185" text-anchor="middle" class="font-mono" font-size="10" fill="var(--color-accent-blue)">B</text>
			</svg>
			<svg viewBox="0 0 300 200" class="mt-2 w-full" role="img">
				<text x="150" y="16" text-anchor="middle" class="font-mono" font-size="11" fill="var(--color-ink-soft)">after CUPED</text>
				<path d="M40,170 C53,170 62,90 85,80 C108,90 117,170 130,170" fill="none" stroke="var(--color-ink-soft)" stroke-width="2" opacity="0.9" />
				<path d="M170,170 C183,170 192,90 215,80 C238,90 247,170 260,170" fill="none" stroke="var(--color-accent-blue)" stroke-width="2" opacity="0.9" />
				<line x1="85" y1="170" x2="85" y2="80" stroke="var(--color-ink-soft)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="2 3" />
				<line x1="215" y1="170" x2="215" y2="80" stroke="var(--color-accent-blue)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="2 3" />
				<text x="85" y="185" text-anchor="middle" class="font-mono" font-size="10" fill="var(--color-ink-soft)">A</text>
				<text x="215" y="185" text-anchor="middle" class="font-mono" font-size="10" fill="var(--color-accent-blue)">B</text>
			</svg>
			<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
				Same two arms, same real gap between them. Before adjustment the two distributions are wide enough to
				overlap heavily, hard to tell apart. After adjustment they're narrower, the same real difference just
				became much easier to see.
			</p>
		</figure>

		<figure>
			<figcaption class="font-display text-lg font-semibold">What CUPED actually subtracts</figcaption>
			<svg viewBox="0 0 300 200" class="mt-3 w-full" role="img" aria-label="A scatter plot of pre-period value against outcome showing a clear upward trend, next to the same points with that trend removed, now scattered flatly.">
				<text x="150" y="16" text-anchor="middle" class="font-mono" font-size="10" fill="var(--color-ink-soft)">pre-period value (x) vs. outcome (y)</text>
				<line x1="58.3" y1="117.5" x2="241.7" y2="42.5" stroke="var(--color-accent-yellow)" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="3 3" />
				<g fill="var(--color-accent-blue)">
					{
						[
							[106.9, 111.5], [80.5, 113.2], [102.3, 91.9], [131.6, 77.9], [146.2, 90.2], [183.7, 80.5],
							[171.3, 68.7], [127.1, 80.8], [209.7, 49.5], [115.3, 107.7], [194.1, 52.0], [185.7, 62.8],
							[107.6, 106.9], [200.6, 42.7], [123.7, 121.9], [125.2, 90.5], [152.3, 88.6], [168.6, 67.2],
							[113.1, 99.3], [183.0, 82.6], [182.9, 81.2], [118.7, 91.7],
						].map(([x, y]) => <circle cx={x} cy={y} r="2.6" opacity="0.85" />)
					}
				</g>
			</svg>
			<svg viewBox="0 0 300 200" class="mt-2 w-full" role="img">
				<text x="150" y="16" text-anchor="middle" class="font-mono" font-size="10" fill="var(--color-ink-soft)">what's left after removing the trend</text>
				<line x1="30" y1="85" x2="270" y2="85" stroke="var(--color-line)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="2 3" />
				<g fill="var(--color-ink)">
					{
						[
							[66.9, 95.3], [40.5, 81.5], [62.3, 68.0], [91.6, 68.2], [106.2, 92.4], [143.7, 103.0],
							[131.3, 80.7], [87.1, 69.1], [169.7, 79.8], [75.3, 95.6], [154.1, 73.5], [145.7, 82.0],
							[67.6, 89.9], [160.6, 65.9], [83.7, 118.4], [85.2, 80.1], [112.3, 94.1], [128.6, 77.1],
							[73.1, 83.8], [143.0, 105.1], [142.9, 103.3], [78.7, 77.6],
						].map(([x, y]) => (
							<circle cx={x + 90} cy={y} r="2.6" opacity="0.85" />
						))
					}
				</g>
			</svg>
			<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
				A real (small-sample) example: pre-period value clearly predicts the outcome, that diagonal trend. Fit and
				subtract it, and what's left has no trend at all, only the part of the outcome pre-period data couldn't
				have told you, less spread out than the raw outcome was.
			</p>
		</figure>
	</div>

	<div class="mt-10 border border-line p-6">
		<p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-accent-blue">Why this doesn't cheat</p>
		<p class="mt-2 max-w-[65ch] text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
			The adjustment subtracts a constant (each visitor's covariate compared to the overall average covariate),
			the same rule for every visitor regardless of which arm they're in. Subtracting the same rule from both arms
			can't manufacture a difference that wasn't there, or hide one that was, it only removes variation the
			pre-period data already explained. That's why it doesn't inflate false positives (see Key Findings), it
			just makes the real answer easier to see, faster.
		</p>
	</div>
</section>
